==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|solicitour||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|soliciteur}}. {{surf|+suf|en|solicit|or}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/səˈlɪsɪtɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/səˈlɪsɪtə/|q2=contracted|/səˈlɪstə/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-solicitor.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{hyph|en|so|li|ci|tor}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who [[solicit]]s, [[particularly]]:
## {{c|en|United Kingdom|Commonwealth}} A [[lawyer]] [[offer]]ing [[most]] [[legal]] [[service]]s to [[client]]s apart from [[representation]] in [[court]], for which the lawyer [[typically]] [[solicit]]s and [[instruct]]s a [[barrister]] [[instead]], although [[rights of audience]] [[vary]] by [[jurisdiction]].
##* {{quote-av|en|date=September 12 1999|author={{w|Anthony Horowitz}}; et al|chapter=Dead Man's Eleven|title={{w|Midsomer Murders}}
|passage=''Stephen Cavendish:'' I'm not going to say any more until I've spoken to my '''solicitor'''. <br> ''DCI Tom Barnaby:'' That might be a good idea, sir.}}
##: {{syn|en|transactional attorney}}
##: {{ux|en|Traditional '''solicitors''' are still found in the United Kingdom, Ireland, New Zealand, India, Hong Kong, Singapore, and parts of Australia.}}
## {{lb|en|Canada|Australia}} A [[lawyer]] [[now]] [[perform]]ing [[both]] the [[role]] of a [[traditional]] solicitor and that of a [[barrister]].
## {{lb|en|US}} The [[chief]] [[legal]] [[officer]] of a [[city]], [[town]], or [[similar]] [[jurisdiction]] in [[some]] [[American]] [[state]]s.
## A [[customer]], a [[requester]] of [[good]]s and [[service]]s, [[especially]] [[john]]s, the [[client]]s of [[prostitute]]s.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=K. Stanton|title=Shakespeare's 'Whores': Erotics, Politics, and Poetics
|passage={{...}} sex workers are regarded as born to be punished; the '''solicitors''' of their services are not.}}
## {{lb|en|North America}} A [[door-to-door]] [[salesperson]], a [[person]] [[solicit]]ing [[sale]]s by [[visiting]] [[other]]s' [[house]]s.
##: {{ux|en|Did you see the sign that says 'No '''Solicitors'''{{'}}?}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} An [[agent]] or [[proxy]] in [[other]] [[context]]s, [[especially]] a [[petitioner]], an [[intercessor]], a [[go-between]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1749|author=Henry Fielding|title=Tom Jones|passage=“I beg, Mr Allworthy,” answered Sophia, “you will not insist on my reasons;—{{...}} Your nephew, sir, hath many virtues—he hath great virtues, Mr Allworthy. I question not but he will do you honour in the world, and make you happy.”—“I wish I could make him so, madam,” replied Allworthy; “but that I am convinced is only in your power. It is that conviction which hath made me so earnest a '''solicitor''' in his favour.”}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[catalyst]], a [[thing]] [[prompt]]ing [[some]] [[action]] or [[behavior]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[tempter]], an [[inciter]].
## {{lb|en|rare|obsolete}} A [[military]] [[recruiter]].

